      "residual": "a survivor with no marker, no handle and no pid AOS ever held: a descendant that double-forks, re-sessions, clears its environment, closes every inherited descriptor and changes directory is in none of the three scans (ancestry at 200 ms, process group, survivor sweep). Measured and accepted rather than missed: a review reproduced it with poll 1 holding the root pid and poll 2 holding a live pid reparented to init and regrouped to itself, and this record still reports process_enforced. Anything AOS did hold a pid for -- including the canary's own escapee, which sheds every marker on purpose -- is killed and checked, and a live one is a detected leak that blocks. What holds the rest is the boundary itself, inherited across fork and exec and unsheddable with the markers, proved per run by that escapee being refused a write outside the workspace. Such a process can outlive the run and write inside that run's own workspace, and what that can do to the measurement is bounded rather than assumed: grading reads a copy of that workspace taken at the moment execution was declared settled, the copy is certified against the source by a digest taken over the copy itself -- so a write made and reverted while the copy was being taken changes the copy's digest and withholds -- and a write after settlement is reported under its own reason. Where the digest cannot cover the tree at all (an entry or a total above lib/digest.mjs's limits, a symlink, an unreadable entry) nothing is certified and the run is withheld rather than scored. It cannot reach anything else, and it cannot see another run"
    },
